# The shed end, on its own camera.
#
# `rig_structure.loom`'s own `GOLDEN` row sits low off the north-west corner
# so the deck, bulwark, piles and seam all read in one frame (see that
# scene's header) — and from there the shed is a sliver at the right edge of
# a 320x200 frame, the roof's corrugation is a handful of pixels, and the
# mast and bollards do not clear visibility at all. `loom render` draws
# exactly one camera per scene (`World::active_camera` takes the first
# `Camera` node and there is no flag to name another), so getting a second
# view of the same rig means a second scene file, the same choice
# `rig_deck.loom` already made for the deck field alone rather than
# reframing this file. Neither `rig_deck` nor `rig_structure` has a
# committed reference yet (`tests/references/` has no `rig_*.png`), but
# repointing `rig_structure`'s own camera would still conflate two different
# proofs on one row the moment one is blessed — cheaper to keep them apart
# from the start.
#
# It carries `Deck` for footing — a shed with no ground under it in the
# frame reads as a diorama piece, not as part of the rig — plus the five
# nodes Task 4 added: `Shed`, `ShedRoof`, `Mast`, `BollardWest`,
# `BollardEast`. Positions, half-extents and materials are copied verbatim
# from `rig_structure.loom`, which copied them from the generators' own
# stdout — see that scene's header for the table. Piles, seam and bulwark
# are left out: nothing about them is new here, and `rig_structure` already
# proves they load.
#
# **The shed's north face sits at world z = 2.600** (center z 4.60 minus its
# 2.000 z half-extent) — `Mirror`/`MirrorFrame`'s mount in `deeper_demo.loom`.
# Neither is built or added to this scene; nothing on the shed or its roof
# may ever project past that plane. Same note as `rig_structure.loom`'s
# header, repeated here because this is the file whoever touches the shed
# next is most likely to have open.
#
# **Camera** stands on the deck, inboard of the bulwark rail rather than
# outside it — put outside, at deck height, the rail line sits between the
# lens and the shed's own base and cuts the sill off in every framing that
# was tried. From inboard, at roughly cap height, looking south-east across
# the mast toward the shed's west gable and roof, nothing between the camera
# and the shed occludes it, and the shed's board siding, its roof's
# corrugation and its rust streaking are all readable at 320x200. See the
# task report for the closer shot and what it settles about the rot texture.

[scene]
format = 1
id = "a7b8a1fe-d059-496d-bc58-209fe6dee629"

[[asset]]
key = "deck_timber"
id = "67fb25df-f9d5-4b06-b4ab-b5b5b19bf77d"
